last time we covered how you can use  google cloud platform metrics as service  level indicators  to measure the reliability of your  services and set reliability targets  using slos  but what do you do if you need custom  telemetry for your slis today  i'm going to show you how to use  prometheus and open senses to write  custom metrics  and define slis and slos using them  welcome to engineering for reliability  with google cloud  imagine a basic web application  architecture like this while we can use  the metrics emitted by the load balancer  to track things like request counts and  response latency  what if our application running on gk  needs to keep track of specific  transaction details  and write a custom counter for them what  if we want to use this kind of custom  telemetry as our sli for the service  this is where custom metrics come in  today i'm going to show you how to use  two open source approaches to custom  metric telemetry  the first one is prometheus prometheus  is an open source systems monitoring the  learning toolkit  originally built at soundcloud it has a  very active developer and user community  and joined the cloud native computing  foundation back in 2016.  this is the architecture prometheus uses  to collect metrics the general idea is  that your service needs to expose a  dedicated metrics endpoint  which prometheus will then scrape to  collect metric data that data is stored  in a time series database and can be  queried through a web ui or an api  we'll link to the full code sample in  the show notes let's have a look at how  to instrument an application written in  node.js  to emit counter and histogram metrics  start by defining the metrics themselves  for each specify the metric type its  name and help message that will describe  what the metric is capturing  to track latency use a histogram and  call its observe method  to increment counters call their ink  method finally  create a dedicated metrics endpoint in  your application that prometheus will  use to scrape for metrics  the instrumentation is done now if you  go to the slash metrics endpoint in your  app  you'll see a page that looks like this  the metrics and their values are written  out here  now you need to configure prometheus  itself to scrape this endpoint  you do that by adding some annotations  in the deployment spec in your  application  enable scraping and define the port and  end point where the metrics are  available  next you need to ingest these metrics  into cloud monitoring  we'll link the detailed instructions in  the show notes but the basic idea is  that you patch your prometheus server  with a collector  that will send these metrics to cloud  monitoring once your metrics are  available there  you're ready to use them for slis and  slos here's an example of how you can  use a latency metric written by  prometheus  to create a distribution cut sli and set  an slo target for it  a distribution cut allows you to capture  the percentage of values that fall  within a specified threshold  as defined by range this slo expects  that 98  of responses complete within 500  milliseconds over a rolling 28 day  period  prometheus is a popular open source  monitoring framework and it's well  suited for monitoring applications  running in gke  but what if you don't want to maintain  that additional infrastructure  in that case you can use open sensors to  write metrics directly  using the cloud monitoring exporter open  census is a single distribution of  libraries that collect metrics and  distributed traces from your services  let's have a look at how to instrument  your service configure the cloud  monitoring exporter  and use open systems metrics to define  slides and slos  again we'll link to the full code  samples in the show notes but let's take  a quick look at instrumenting an  application written in node.js  to emit counter and histogram metrics  start by configuring the cloud  monitoring exporter this will send the  metrics you capture directly to cloud  monitoring  where you'll then use them to create  slis and define slos  next define a measure for each metric  measures describe how the data points  themselves will be recorded create a  view for each measure  views are basically the time skills  you'll be adding data points to  they also include information about how  that data will be aggregated  for our latency histogram you need to  specify your bucket boundaries  it's important to at least make sure  that you have buckets that capture  requests that are going to fall within  and outside of your slo however you'll  likely want more than just those to  allow you to for example measure p99  latency  finally record values using the record  method unlike prometheus this is the  same for counters and histograms and  open senses  you won't need to configure any scraping  the exporter will write the values  directly to cloud monitoring  you can then use them to define slos  like this here's an example of a  distribution cut sli  using the response latency metric  written by open census  with an slo of 98 of responses being  completed in under 1 000 milliseconds  thanks for joining me today in this  episode we looked at how to instrument  your code to write custom metrics of  open census at prometheus  use them to define service level  indicators and set service level  objectives  against those you're now ready to start  defining slis by using these open source  libraries  in your own services in our next episode  we'll look at alerting on our slos with  air budget burn rates  don't forget to like and subscribe to  never miss out on more engineering for  reliability with google cloud  see you soon  you
 

